faq:
  - q: "What are the most important things to set up before a video interview?"
    a: "Four things: (1) Camera at eye level — laptop propped or external webcam. Looking down at a camera reads as low energy and submissive. (2) Lighting — face should be lit from the front, not backlit. Natural light from a window in front of you, or a ring light. (3) Background — clean, uncluttered. Neutral wall or bookshelf. Avoid virtual backgrounds if possible — they signal weak setup. (4) Stable internet plus headphones with microphone. Test everything the day before using a video call with someone you know."
  - q: "How do I come across confidently on video?"
    a: "Look at the camera, not the screen — this creates the impression of eye contact. Sit slightly forward, not slumped back. Speak 10–15% slower than you normally would — screens compress energy and fast speech reads as nervous. Pause briefly before answering questions — video compression makes gaps seem shorter to the interviewer than to you."
  - q: "What is the most common video interview mistake?"
    a: "Looking at your own face or at the interviewer's face on screen, not at the camera. The result is that you appear to be looking down or sideways — it reads as evasive or disengaged even when you are not. Practice directing your gaze to the camera dot, not the screen."
  - q: "How do I handle technical issues during a video interview?"
    a: "Stay calm and transparent. 'I'm having audio issues — can you hear me?' is better than pushing through hoping they can. Have the interviewer's email or phone number ready before the call in case you need to reconnect. A brief technical issue handled professionally often has zero impact on the interview outcome."
